    {
      "@id": "docs/research-and-analysis/index.md#provenance-2",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "issue",
      "cat-harness:section": "Research and analysis",
      "prov:value": "| [Table-of-contents extraction](toc-extraction.html) | Which way of inferring a PDF's table of contents works when the PDF has no outline? Five methods scored on 2026-10-06 against the 13 corpus PDFs that carry one, outline hidden as the answer key. **Layout — a printed contents page, else heading styles from font metrics — reaches title F1 0.88, the consensus of all methods 0.92 — 0.83 on 20 held-out PDFs — against 0.30** for the text patterns it replaced; Grobid measured at 0.59 (CRF models); Nougat handed to an agent with network access (issue #2302) |",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "#2302",
        "2026-10-06"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-06"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "docs/research-and-analysis/index.md#provenance-3",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Research and analysis",
      "prov:value": "| [Forced along a BPMN vs managing it](bpmn-forced-vs-managed.html) | When the same BPMN diagram (`test-plan-execution.bpmn` + `qa-report-signing.bpmn`) is run over the same 10-case task bank under token-machine step enforcement (`forced`) vs handing the full diagram in context (`self-managed`), do outcomes change, and at what compute? (bean `folio-assistant-502c`). **Not yet answered: the two-arm runner exists, but so far (2026-10-26) it has been run only with a simulated agent (a hand-written step script), not a model.** That run shows the token machine refuses every scripted shortcut, the forced arm's per-turn prompt is about 31× smaller (byte-count estimate), and the `folio-test-run/v1` two-hash split works; its outcome rates hold by construction and are not evidence about models |",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-10-26",
        "bean folio-assistant-502c"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-26"
      ]
